1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,284
About two hundred and thirty-four million years ago,

2
00:00:03,284 --> 00:00:07,382
Earth's climate turned wet, on and off, for up to two million years.

3
00:00:07,883 --> 00:00:13,915
The likely trigger was a string of giant volcanic eruptions in what's now western North America,

4
00:00:13,915 --> 00:00:15,046
called Wrangellia.

5
00:00:15,046 --> 00:00:18,878
They pumped out huge amounts of carbon and heated the planet.

6
00:00:19,383 --> 00:00:21,221
It wasn't one endless storm.

7
00:00:21,221 --> 00:00:25,617
Scientists see several pulses, swinging from dry to humid and back.

8
00:00:26,117 --> 00:00:28,187
Like and subscribe for more deep time.

9
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:31,427
Around a third of marine species may have died out.

10
00:00:31,917 --> 00:00:35,991
Then, in rocks in Italy's Dolomites, the footprints change.

11
00:00:35,991 --> 00:00:38,477
First no dinosaur tracks, then lots.

12
00:00:38,477 --> 00:00:42,966
Many scientists think this wet crisis helped dinosaurs take over.

13
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:46,809
Conifers, modern-style coral reefs and plankton spread too.

14
00:00:47,317 --> 00:00:50,077
Today, warming is melting Earth's ice instead.

15
00:00:50,077 --> 00:00:51,577
What if all of it melted?

16
00:00:51,577 --> 00:00:53,138
Full story on the channel.
